*This is My Ledger, the personal side of v64otd.com. This series is history first: what happened, with sources. Where I give my own opinion, it's marked.*

**Faction:** the Founders' word for what we'd call a party or special-interest bloc. James Madison defined it as a group of citizens, whether majority or minority, united by a shared passion or interest that runs counter to the rights of others or the good of the whole community.

### What They Believed About Liberty

The men who built this country agreed on one thing above almost everything else: liberty is fragile, and power always wants more of it.

They also agreed that factions were one of the biggest threats to it. In *Federalist No. 10*, published in November 1787 to persuade New Yorkers to ratify the Constitution, James Madison put the problem plainly. "The latent causes of faction are thus sown in the nature of man," he wrote. People will always split into groups over money, religion, and power.

Madison's answer is the key to understanding everything that came after. He said there were two ways to deal with faction: "by removing its causes" or "by controlling its effects." Removing the causes would mean destroying liberty itself, because "Liberty is to faction what air is to fire." You can't put out the fire without taking away the air everyone breathes.

So Madison concluded that "the causes of faction cannot be removed; and that relief is only to be sought in the means of controlling its effects." That's what the Constitution was built to do. A large republic with many competing interests, power split between the federal government and the states, and three branches checking each other would keep any one faction from taking over.

*Editorial:* Notice what Madison was counting on: lots of factions, constantly shifting, none strong enough to run everything. What he didn't plan for was two factions so big and so permanent that they'd divide the whole country between them. That's what we have now.

### "I Would Not Go There at All"

Thomas Jefferson was even more blunt. In March 1789, writing from Paris to his friend Francis Hopkinson, he described joining a party as surrendering your own judgment. "Such an addiction is the last degradation of a free and moral agent," he wrote. "If I could not go to heaven but with a party, I would not go there at all."

Within three years, Jefferson was helping lead one.

### How It Happened So Fast

The split came over money and power, specifically Treasury Secretary Alexander Hamilton's plan for the new nation's finances. Hamilton wanted a strong central government, a national bank, and federal power to manage the country's debts. Jefferson, then secretary of state, and Madison, then a congressman, believed that plan concentrated too much power in too few hands.

Neither side handled it the way the Founders' own warnings advised.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; **The newspapers went to war.** John Fenno's *Gazette of the United States* promoted Hamilton's program. In 1791, Philip Freneau launched the rival *National Gazette* with the backing of Jefferson and Madison. Jefferson gave Freneau a job as a translating clerk at the State Department, at $250 a year, while Freneau ran a paper attacking the administration Jefferson served in. Madison wrote nineteen anonymous essays for it, many of which criticized Washington's administration and argued for the formation of political parties.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; **They started calling each other a faction.** In May 1792, Hamilton wrote to a friend that "Mr. Madison cooperating with Mr. Jefferson is at the head of a faction decidedly hostile to me and my administration," driven by views "subversive of the principles of good government and dangerous to the union."

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; **Madison changed his tune.** Five years after warning about faction in *Federalist No. 10*, Madison published "A Candid State of Parties" in September 1792. In it, he called division into parties "natural to most political societies," and cast his side as the party that believed "mankind are capable of governing themselves" and the other as one "more partial to the opulent."

In about five years, the men who wrote the warnings had built the first party machines: newspapers, organizers and a common enemy.

### Washington's Last Warning

George Washington watched it happen from the presidency and hated it. When he left office in 1796, his Farewell Address spent much of its length on the danger of parties.

"The alternate domination of one faction over another, sharpened by the spirit of revenge, natural to party dissension," Washington warned, "is itself a frightful despotism." He said parties could become "potent engines, by which cunning, ambitious, and unprincipled men will be enabled to subvert the power of the people and to usurp for themselves the reins of government."

John Adams had written something similar in 1780: "There is nothing which I dread so much as a division of the republic into two great parties." He called it "the greatest political evil under our Constitution."

Then Adams became president, and his party proved them both right.

### When the Party in Power Jailed Its Critics

In 1798, with John Adams in office and his Federalists controlling Congress, the Federalists passed the Sedition Act. It made it a crime to publish "false, scandalous and malicious writing" about the federal government.

In practice, it was used against their political opponents. More than two dozen people were convicted, most of them publishers who supported Jefferson's side.

The most famous was Matthew Lyon, a congressman from Vermont. He was convicted, sentenced to four months in jail, and fined $1,000. His constituents reelected him while he sat in his cell.

The Sedition Act expired when Adams left office in 1801. Its unpopularity is widely seen as one reason Jefferson beat Adams in 1800.

### The Election That Wrote Parties Into the Constitution

That election of 1800 showed how far the parties had already gone.

Under the original Constitution, electors cast two votes for president, and the runner-up became vice president. Jefferson's party wanted Jefferson as president and Aaron Burr as vice president, but its electors failed to coordinate. Jefferson and Burr tied, 73 electoral votes each.

The tie went to the House of Representatives, which took 36 ballots over about a week. It ended on February 17, 1801, after several Federalists abstained. Hamilton, Jefferson's longtime rival, reportedly told Federalists that Jefferson was less dangerous than Burr.

The fix was the Twelfth Amendment, in place for the 1804 election. It had electors vote separately for president and vice president. In other words, the country amended the Constitution to accommodate party tickets. The document the Founders wrote with no mention of parties now had to be rewritten around them.

### So Was It the Federalists?

Here's the honest answer: **it was both of them.**

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; **The Federalists** used the power of government to punish their critics. The Sedition Act jailed opposition editors and a sitting congressman.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; **The Jefferson-Madison side** built the first organized opposition party: a party newspaper backed by a State Department job, anonymous essays, and a deliberate effort to unite everyone who opposed Hamilton.

One side abused power. The other organized to take it. Both were led by men who had warned against doing exactly that.

> *Editorial:* I don't think they were hypocrites in the cheap sense. I think they found out what Madison already knew: faction is human nature. They each believed that the other side was the real threat to liberty, and that organizing was the only way to stop it. That's the same thing both parties tell us today. Every generation since has been told the other side is too dangerous to allow anything else. The trap was set in the 1790s. We've been living in it ever since.

What the Founders got right was the warning. What they couldn't do was follow it. In Part 3, we'll see how the next generation turned their reluctant parties into machines, and how, at the height of the machine era, more Americans voted than at almost any time since.

### Discussion Questions for the Comments

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Madison’s answer to faction was more competition, not fewer choices. What would it take to give voters more than two real options?

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Was the Sedition Act worse than anything either party does now?

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Would you have sided with Hamilton or Jefferson in 1792?

